A||||8||la9||Port EGR valve location||||1||
Z||000000||la9||02-13-2000||12:03 PM||||Could someone tell me where the port EGR valve plugs into ? I have the 2 vacuum hoses from the bottom hooked up but where does the one at the top go ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||la9||02-15-2000||01:54 AM||||No one can open up their lid and tell me where this plugs in at ?????????????????<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||02-15-2000||02:10 AM||||Could you be more descriptive of your issue, V6 or L4, approximate location of the lines you are referring to, etc?||||reg||1
Z||000003||la9||02-15-2000||11:35 AM||||I sent you an e-mail. This is only on the V6 and is located around the thermostat housing. I have a diagram of it if anyone wants it or can post it for me. I don't have time right now to figure out how to post pictures.<BR>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||02-15-2000||12:29 PM||||the line that comes off the EGR valve goes to the EGR solenoid. A small black tube like piece of plastic. one tube goes to that ant the tube off that goes to the vapor canister all the way on the other side of the engine bay.  so to answer your question the tube directly off the EGR goes to the EGR solenoid. This often goes bad and cn be by-passed take the vacuum tube from the EGR and T it in to the smallest vacuum tube on the vapor canister. This ONLY pulls a vacuum at part throttlee just like the dead solenoid. i did it to my car just to pass emissions cause this solenoid was thrown away...||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||02-15-2000||01:32 PM||||Upon receiving an excellent description of the exact hose in question, I have easily identified this as the worthless piece of tubing that attaches to the EGR vacuum solenoid filter.  This is the air intake that the EGR solenoid uses for fresh air when it vents the system to close the EGR valve.  Normally, this tube was routed back to the air cleaner assembly via the small black metal pipe located on the firewall.  Since the air is filtered anyway (ie.. EGR solenoid filter), there is no real reason why this cannot draw air directly (eg. without the tube attached).  I would leave it to the owner to determine if this tube should remain, since those that desire to restore everthing to "exact" stock appearance may have reason to keep this tube in place.  I killed mine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTBro||02-15-2000||04:36 PM||||I am also having problems with my EGR valve.  Does anybody know where the vacuum comes from?  There is no suction or vacuum in my solenoid, therefore, no EGR operation.<P>PS.  Why did they have to make the EGR system run by vacuum lines?  Wouldn't it be better if it was electrical?||||reg||5
Z||000007||DRH||02-15-2000||05:26 PM||||87GTBro, <P>Just to clarify... you may already know this...<P>The EGR <B>Valve</B> is located on the drivers side of the engine mounted on the EGR <B>Tube</B> which runs between the exhaust crossover pipe and the under side of the intake plenum. The vacuum to this valve is controlled by the ECM using the EGR <B>Solenoid</B> which is mounted on the passenger side of the engine near the coolant fill cap.<P>You won't have vacuum at the EGR Valve at idle. You should have vacuum at one of the lines running to the EGR Solenoid at idle.<BR>I'm not sure of the exact routing. It may be shown on the emissions sticker on the inside of the decklid.<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||02-16-2000||05:56 PM||||To add to what DRH said... The actual source for the EGR vacuum (before it gets to the solenoid) is a fitting on the bottom of the throttle body. It is "ported" vacuum. You shouldn't see much vacuum (if any) at idle. ||||reg||1
